\rev{The history argument also determines the domain of the observables. For a deterministic retarded differential equation with maximum delay $\tau_{\max}$, the natural state is the segment $\bm\eta_t(\theta)=\bm x(t+\theta)$, $-\tau_{\max}\leq\theta\leq0$, in the function space $C([-\tau_{\max},0],\mathbb R^{n_x})$ \citep{Hale1993FDE}. If the autonomous dynamics generate a well-defined solution map $\Phi^{\Delta t}$, the Koopman operator acts on functions of that segment by $(\mathcal U^{\Delta t}g)(\bm\eta)=g(\Phi^{\Delta t}\bm\eta)$. A finite history interval is therefore distinct from a finite set of samples: for sampled continuous-time delay dynamics, Equation~\eqref{eq:delayed-process} does not imply that the available samples determine an exact closed transition. The learned predictor approximates that dependence using $\mathcal S_k$ below.}

\rev{Explicit delayed terms are valid candidate observables of this history state, for example $g_{i,\ell}(\bm\eta_t)=\eta_{t,i}(-\ell\Delta t)=x_i(t-\ell\Delta t)$ for $0\leq\ell\Delta t\leq\tau_{\max}$. Delay-coordinate constructions provide a systematic route to history-based Koopman approximations \citep{Kamb2020DelayObservables,Pan2020TimeDelay}. For an exactly finite-memory discrete model $\bm x_{k+1}=f(\bm x_k,\ldots,\bm x_{k-q})$ with $q\geq1$, the full stack $\bm y_k=[\bm x_k^\top,\ldots,\bm x_{k-q}^\top]^\top$ obeys the Markov update
\[
 \bm y_{k+1}=G(\bm y_k)
 =\begin{bmatrix}f(\bm x_k,\ldots,\bm x_{k-q})\\ \bm x_k\\ \vdots\\ \bm x_{k-q+1}\end{bmatrix}.
\]
Only the shift rows are automatically linear. A dictionary $\bm\varphi$ evolves exactly under a finite matrix $\bm K$ only when its span is Koopman-invariant, equivalently $\bm\varphi(G(\bm y))=\bm K\bm\varphi(\bm y)$ throughout the relevant domain \citep{Brunton2016Invariant}. Merely adding delayed coordinates, or nonlinear functions of them, does not establish this closure. Selecting only a few physical-delay coordinates is also distinct from retaining the full stack needed for its shift update.}

\rev{The physical delay and a reconstruction lag serve different purposes. A known physical lag identifies a specific delayed process variable; reconstruction lags are chosen to retain predictive information and need not equal that lag. Takens' embedding result concerns generic pairs of a smooth diffeomorphism and a scalar observation on a compact finite-dimensional manifold \citep{Takens1981}. It does not by itself establish a sufficient sample window for an arbitrary delay equation or a closed linear dictionary. With varying inputs, the transition must additionally be conditioned on the input, or an autonomous Koopman formulation must augment the state with the input sequence and its shift \citep{Korda2018}. Here past inputs enter $\mathcal S_k$, and future inputs are prescribed explicitly; the additive $\bm B\bm u$ term is a chosen predictor structure, not a general exact representation of input-dependent dynamics.}

